the new m1 mac chip from apple has been  blowing people's minds  because of how good the performance and  efficiency has been  and has been doing it while using the  arm v8 isa  or instruction set architecture which  was originally released  10 long years ago but just a couple of  weeks ago  arm announced the new version 9 isa  which will be the foundation for the  next decade of arm-based chips  so in this video i'm going to explain  all of the advantages of  rnv9 over the current v8 and then i'm  going to discuss  what this means for apple silicon max  and why this is such a huge deal for  apple  first off i'm 100 sure that apple is  working on designing  new apple silicon chips based on the new  arm v9 architecture  because apple was actually the very  first company  to design a chip based on the rmv8  architecture  which was released 10 years ago the main  benefit of rnv8 was that it switched  from using a 32-bit architecture  to 64-bit and shortly after it was  released  apple announced the a7 chip the very  first  64-bit iphone chip that went into the  iphone 5s  and this basically sparked a rally of  significant year-over-year performance  increases  with each and every new a series chip  from apple  all thanks to the new 64-bit arm  v8 architecture which eventually led to  the m1 mac  chip that we have today now before i get  into explaining the improvements of the  new rv9 instruction set architecture  i want to explain what that actually  means in the first place  so here's a simple explanation of modern  cpu instruction sets and how they work  by android authorities robert triggs  every computer out there  requires a cpu or processor  and cpus only work when they are given  very specific instructions  which is called the instruction set  traditional computer cpus like the ones  made by intel  currently use the x86 instruction set  which is a sisk design which stands for  complex instruction set computer cisc  cpus offer and support  many instructions which are very complex  since they can execute multiple  operations at once  among other factors but the downside to  this is that these  types of cisc cpus consume a ton of  power  which is fine when you're hooked up to  an outlet but it becomes a problem for  devices with  batteries like smartphones and that's  why there's  risk the reduced instruction set  computer architecture  risk cpus are much more simple since  they have a limited amount of  instructions  but the benefit of that is that they use  much less power  and that's why every single smartphone  built today uses a risk-based chip  so it gets as much battery life as  possible including the iphone  which has been using arm-based chips  which are based on risk  ever since the original iphone in 2017  apple released the iphone 7 with the a10  fusion chip  of course based on arm which was the  first iphone chip to feature both  performance cores  and efficiency cores using arm's new  dynam iq big little technology  which basically revolutionized the  industry because you can get  really high performance and great  battery life  within the same chip and that is exactly  what apple's  m1 chip uses for high performance cores  and four efficiency cores but the most  impressive part is that apple was able  to use  a risc based cpu design in a mac  with awesome software support and  performance that beats out  many complex cisc based chips which many  people  did not think was possible because risk  chips  were meant for simple workloads and not  mac computer workloads  but apple obviously nailed it and it's  about to get  even better thanks to arm v9 so let's  get into the new features  and the main advantages when arm  revealed v9  just a couple of weeks ago they claim  that users can expect a cpu performance  increase of 30 percent  over the next two generations of their  cpus which you can see right here in  this chart  thirty percent in the next two years and  the way they're gonna do it is by  offering new methods  to reduce memory latency and improve the  cpu frequency  bandwidth and cache so apple's own  arm-based chip designs  should be able to take advantage of  those new methods of increasing  performance  even without having to increase core  count thanks to rv9 but by far  the most important change for apple is  the introduction of  sve2 as the baseline feature of rnv9  cpus  sve stands for scalable vector  extensions  so let me explain what all of that means  currently the  m1 mac chip relies on neon which only  supports  single vector register instruction  lengths of 128 bits  so as you can see in this chart  comparing the performance of various  modes  of matrix computations the m1 chip on  the right  cannot even run some of the complex  tests because they exceed  the single 128-bit vector length that  neon  is limited to and for the orange bar  which uses a quad  32-bit instruction extension the m1 chip  uses  a special compiler that can convert it  into  a single 128-bit instruction  which it can then run but as you can see  the other x86 chips can easily run  more complex tasks like some of the 256  bit modes  or even 512 bit modes which require a  lot more power to run  so since the m1 chip currently only  supports 128 bit lengths  this creates a pretty huge issue for  apple especially if they want to release  an  apple silicon mac pro which will be used  by professionals  who need to run high-end and very  complex  scientific and mathematical workloads  which work  using those higher bit lengths and that  is where rmv 9's new  sve2 feature comes in which is a  scalable extension  with no fixed width sve 2 is able to  scale  vectors ranging anywhere from 128 to  2048 bits  down into 128 bit blocks which it can  then  process this will essentially allow  apple's future  m series chips to run those complex  tasks  that require wider bit lengths and apple  can even optimize their arm chips  and software for various bit lengths  like 128 or 256  for the apple watch or iphone all the  way up to 1024 or 2048  for a high-end mac pro which will  optimize it  for those very complex workloads so you  can bet  that the apple silicon mac pro will have  an  arm v9 based chip no doubt about it now  moving on  rv9 is also going to improve security  which apple is all about rmv9 introduces  a new  confidential compute architecture or cca  which creates realms which are secure  environments that various parts of the  code  run in that are separated from the os  and  the hypervisor so this essentially means  that your apps will run in a much more  secure state  so your sensitive data won't be able to  be accessed by the host machine  the hardware manufacturer the service  provider  or other software running on the device  or sharing the machine  and along with that rv9 is adding memory  tag  extensions which also help security by  identifying spatial and temporal memory  security issues  in software so to sum up the main  benefits of arm v9  it's gonna improve performance by up to  30 percent in two generations  it's gonna improve security and it's  going to allow arm-based chips to run  more complex workloads that use higher  bit lengths  now with all of that said i want to get  into what this means for apple  and how i think this is going to impact  the future apple silicon max  way back when apple announced the  transition to apple silicon  in june of 2020 and intact posted an  article saying that apple's transition  is a bit of an odd one because arm was  expected to announce their rv9  architecture  which they only just did a couple of  weeks ago but back then  either of two things was going to happen  either arm was literally just about to  announce  rnv9 and apple's mac chips would be the  first ones  on the new architecture or apple's mac  chips  would be stuck with the older arm v8  which we now know  is what ended up happening so here is my  theory on why apple chose  not to wait for rv9 for their very first  m1 max  apple kn